 ## Sessions and the shared seat
 
 - **One seat per pane, one binding per session.** Your session, each other agent session in the pane and each subagent is its own binding with its own read cursors and liveness. Another session in the pane does not replace yours; only a new session of the same process (`/clear`, resume) supersedes its predecessor, and a closed pane retires the seat. A person's `human me init` seat holds only the person's binding, and an agent's first call there ends it.
-- **Codex `/side`.** A native interactive fork registers its own top-level binding and stays connected beside main; ordinary commands use its own session environment. Never export the parent's ID. Background workers remain read-only. Same-seat memberships/receipts are shared, but cursors and ACK attribution stay per binding. The observed side is ephemeral; native `send_message /root` stays within its own tree. An ht send reaches other seats and sibling bindings on the shared seat, including main, through their joined thread; only the exact sending binding is excluded. Sibling mail creates no self ACK obligation. See [Codex setup and limits](../codex/README.md#interactive-side).
+- **Codex `/side`.** A native interactive fork registers its own top-level binding and stays connected beside main; ordinary commands use its own session environment. Never export the parent's ID. Background workers remain read-only. Same-seat memberships/receipts are shared, but cursors and ACK attribution stay per binding. The observed side is ephemeral; native `send_message /root` stays within its own tree. An ht send reaches other seats and sibling bindings on the shared seat, including main, through their joined thread; only the exact sending binding is excluded. Sibling mail creates no self ACK obligation. While a side chat is displayed, a native wake waits until it closes (it is never typed into the side chat); a sender's overdue line shows the refused wake (`wake=`). See [Codex setup and limits](../codex/README.md#interactive-side).
 - **Shared seat.** Memberships, invitations and receipts belong to the seat. A `join` or `leave` by any session applies to every session in the pane, so never `leave` a thread casually on behalf of the pane. The first top-level session to ACK settles a receipt once; a later ACK is a no-op.
 - **Session id required.** Every agent call, reads included, sends the id from your harness environment (`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`; `CODEX_SESSION_ID` with `CODEX_THREAD_ID`; `HERMES_SESSION_ID`; `ANTIGRAVITY_CONVERSATION_ID` for agy; `HERDR_THREADS_OPENCODE_SESSION`, set by the opencode plugin). Run `herdr-threads` from the agent's own shell tool. `session_claim_required` names the missing variable (restart a pre-upgrade agent; stale hooks: ask your user to run `herdr-threads setup`), `liveness_fingerprint_required` means the harness process cannot be fingerprinted, and `managed_daemon_refused` means Codex runs under `codex app-server --managed-daemon` (no pane): keep Codex in its pane or run it with `--no-daemon`.
 - **First contact and fast-forward.** A session new to the seat starts at the seat's furthest-read point. The hook shows `history: #NAME (ID): N earlier messages, M from people (K open requests), last Xm ago` per joined thread (at most 8, then `+N more threads`). A resumed session with more unread than one inbox page shows `history: resumed past N messages:` with per-thread counts and the last message, and skips ahead (pending ACK obligations stay). Catch up with `herdr-threads summary THREAD`.
